<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;" colspan="2"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Contribute to storytelling sessions with simple sentences based on visual prompts. Interactive: Relay short messages from one person to another in a group setting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can contribute simple sentences to a story from a visual prompt (e.g., "The monkey sleeps in the tree."). Can relay short messages or instructions about animals and their habitats to classmates in group activities (e.g., "The sloth is slow."). Can use basic language to exchange information about nature activities, using gestures if needed (e.g., "The cat climbs the tree.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows students large flashcards of rainforest animals (sloth, monkey, toucan, snake, frog) one by one. For each flashcard, the teacher asks, "What animal is it?" Students respond chorally, "It's a [sloth]." The teacher then asks, "Is it slow or fast?" and encourages students to make a "slow" or "fast" gesture. This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target sentences using the flashcards. "This is a <b>slow sloth</b>. It is <b>furry</b>." (Teacher traces a slow movement with hands). "This is a <b>colorful toucan</b>. It can <b>fly</b>." (Teacher flaps arms). The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /s/ in "sloth" and /r/ in "rainforest" while modeling sentences like "The **r**ainfo**r**est has a **s**low **s**loth." The teacher also models simple actions like 'climb', 'sleep', 'fly' with gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses Concept Checking Questions to ensure understanding of key words and phrases.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a sloth flashcard) "Is the sloth noisy or quiet?" (Expected answer: "Quiet!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a toucan flashcard) "Can the toucan climb or fly?" (Expected answer: "Fly!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, visually rich digital story or large picture cards depicting different rainforest animals in their habitats (e.g., sloth sleeping in a tree, monkey swinging on vines, toucan flying). The "text" for the presentation will be spoken by the teacher, focusing on simple descriptions and actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing animals with traits: "The sloth is slow and furry." Talking about preferences: "I like sloths. They are quiet."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher presents the visual story/pictures, narrating short, simple sentences about each animal and its action/trait. For example, "Look! This is a **rainforest**. A **monkey** lives here. The **monkey climbs** the **vines**. It is a **jumpy monkey**." "And this is a **sloth**. The **sloth sleeps** in the **tree**. It is a **slow, furry sloth**." After presenting, the teacher asks simple oral comprehension questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal climbs the vines?" (Expected: "Monkey!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the sloth fly?" (Expected: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where does the monkey live?" (Expected: "Rainforest!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, colorful toucan, wild jaguar), WH-questions ("What animal is it?", "Where does it live?"), and Present simple verbs (live, climb, sleep, fly) with the target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Repetition & Action Miming:</b> The teacher holds up animal flashcards. For each card, the teacher says a descriptive sentence and performs an action, and students repeat chorally and mime the action. E.g., Teacher: "A **slow sloth sleeps**." (Teacher acts slow and sleepy.) Students: "A **slow sloth sleeps**." (Students mimic.) Teacher: "A **jumpy monkey climbs**." (Teacher acts jumpy and climbs.) Students: "A **jumpy monkey climbs**." This reinforces pronunciation of /s/ and /dʒ/ and associated actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion Drill:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard and starts a sentence, then points to different students to complete it orally. E.g., Teacher: (shows a frog) "The frog is..." Student A: "Jumpy!" Teacher: "The frog..." Student B: "lives in the jungle!" Teacher: (shows a jaguar) "This is a..." Student C: "wild jaguar!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Guess My Animal" Pair Work:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small animal picture cards. Student A picks a card secretly and describes the animal using two simple sentences, e.g., "It's slow. It's furry." or "It climbs. It's a monkey." Student B guesses, "Is it a sloth?" or "Is it a monkey?" Students take turns describing and guessing. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation/grammar for accuracy. This activity helps students to exchange simple information about nature using basic language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Rainforest Story Chain"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts a simple story about the rainforest using a visual prompt (e.g., a large picture of a rainforest). For example, "This is the **rainforest**. A **slow sloth** lives here." The teacher then passes an animal flashcard to the next student. That student must add one simple sentence to the story, describing the animal or its action, incorporating an adjective or verb, and then pass another animal flashcard to the next student. E.g., Student 1: "A **monkey climbs** the **vines**." Student 2: "A **colorful parrot flies**." Student 3: "A **snake hides** in the **hole**." Students are encouraged to use gestures if needed. The goal is to build a collaborative story about "It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!" and other rainforest animals, focusing on fluency and communication, even with short utterances. This activity allows students to contribute simple sentences to a story from visual prompts and relay short messages about animals to classmates.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Rainforest Story Chain" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Rainforest Story Chain," contributing at least one simple sentence about an animal from the rainforest based on a visual prompt.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (e.g., clear /s/ in "sloth," /r/ in "rainforest," /dʒ/ in "jungle").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (at least one animal noun, one adjective, or one verb per contribution).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Contribution of a simple, coherent sentence (e.g., "The monkey climbs.", "It's a slow sloth.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in the group activity.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions by giving a thumbs up/down or circling a happy/sad face on a simple worksheet:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I say new animal words?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I tell a sentence about an animal?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite animal to talk about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word about the rainforest did you learn to say?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., student engagement with flashcards, collaborative storytelling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., more sentence frames for weaker students, more focus on specific verb conjugations, stronger emphasis on the initial /j/ sound for "jungle").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to contribute simple sentences and relay messages effectively? How can I further encourage interaction?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job speaking about our rainforest animals today! Next time, in Lesson 4, we will learn how to write simple sentences about these amazing animals and their homes. We will write about the 'slow sloth' and the 'colorful toucan' we talked about."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>